              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The question of whether Crimea belongs to Russia is answered differently depending on whether you look at it from a **legal (*de jure*)** perspective or a **practical (*de facto*)** perspective.\n\n### 1. The International and Ukrainian Perspective (*De Jure* \u2014 By Law)\nAccording to international law and the vast majority of the global community, **Crimea belongs to Ukraine**. \n\n* **International Law:** In March 2014, following the Russian military occupation of the peninsula, the United Nations General Assembly passed Resolution 68/262. Supported by 100 member states, the resolution affirmed Ukraine's territorial integrity and declared the referendum held in Crimea to join Russia invalid. \n* **Global Non-Recognition:** The United Nations, the European Union, the United States, and most countries worldwide do not recognize Russia's claim to Crimea. They view the annexation as an illegal violation of international law, the UN Charter, and bilateral treaties (such as the 1994 Budapest Memorandum, in which Russia pledged to respect Ukraine's existing borders).\n* **Ukraine's Position:** Ukraine considers Crimea to be an integral part of its sovereign territory that is currently under temporary military occupation. Returning Crimea to Ukrainian control remains a primary war aim of the Ukrainian government.\n\n### 2. The Russian Perspective (*De Facto* \u2014 In Practice)\nIn terms of physical control and administration, **Russia has run Crimea since 2014**.\n\n* **Annexation and Administration:** Following the 2014 Ukrainian revolution, Russian forces seized control of the peninsula. A referendum was quickly organized under military oversight, in which local authorities reported that over 96% of voters chose to join Russia. Days later, Russia formally annexed the region, organizing it into two federal subjects: the Republic of Crimea and the federal city of Sevastopol.\n* **Russia's Legal and Historical Argument:** Moscow argues that Crimea's transfer from the Russian Soviet Republic to the Ukrainian Soviet Republic in 1954 (by Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev) was a historical mistake or legally flawed. Russia asserts that the 2014 referendum was a legitimate exercise of the right of self-determination by the local, majority ethnic-Russian population.\n* **Physical Control:** Since 2014, Russia has fully integrated Crimea into its political, judicial, financial, and military infrastructure. Russian currency, passports, laws, and time zones are used on the peninsula. \n\n### Summary\n* **Legally (*de jure*):** Crimea is widely recognized as **part of Ukraine** under temporary Russian occupation.\n* **Practically (*de facto*):** Crimea is **controlled and administered by Russia**, which considers it an inalienable part of its own territory.",
